nodecode-nextdns is a rewrite of shanelord01's shanelord01/hermes-nextdns:

  https://github.com/shanelord01/hermes-nextdns
  commit 6e46460e9d7f4775d247fb1e467532cf42bfc52d
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Shane Lord (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the client. Its one host, https://api.nextdns.io, its
X-Api-Key header and its custom User-Agent, its four read-only tools and the
argument schemas the model reads, the query strings it builds, the device-name
resolution against the profile's own device list, the trimming of a log row to
timestamp, domain, status, device, matched blocklists, protocol and
encryption, the API's own error detail surfaced instead of a bare status code,
the limit floor of ten and the per-endpoint ceilings, and the cap on how many
rows one answer carries -- all reimplemented in Common Lisp on the Nodecode
cell contract. Every endpoint read is still a GET, and nothing here changes a
profile. The /nextdns command answers the same status totals the plugin's
slash command did. The request carries the API key in a header, the name the
section sets, a 2 MiB cap on the answer and the section's timeout; nothing
else leaves the machine.

What was left out: the Hermes dashboard tab -- its settings form, its Test
connection button and its recent-blocks panel. A Nodecode cell takes its
settings from config.jsonc and answers in the conversation, so the API key,
the profile id and the test of both are the section and the first tool call;
the tab's own settings file (~/.hermes/nextdns_settings.json, mode 0600) is
gone with it, and the key lives in an environment variable the section names
or in an inline secret. The plugin's twelve dashboard backend routes became
the same four verbs a person calls from eval.
